        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The first denominator is $2+(X-1)=1+X$ because the tail after its first $2$ repeats.",
          "worked": "The reciprocal term in the first denominator is the same as the reciprocal term in $X$, since both have the same repeating tail. That term is $X-1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}X&=1+\\frac{1}{2+(X-1)}\\\\X&=1+\\frac{1}{1+X}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, solve for $X$ and confirm your hypothesis from part (c) of the preceding Continued Fractions investigation. If starting with this question, first conjecture the limit from successive finite truncations of $[1;2,2,2,\\ldots]$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$X=\\sqrt{2}$, confirming the hypothesis suggested by the finite approximations in Question 1.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}X-1&=\\frac{1}{1+X}\\\\(X-1)(1+X)&=1\\\\X^2-1&=1\\\\X^2&=2\\\\X&=\\pm\\sqrt{2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe continued fraction is positive, so $X=\\sqrt{2}$. This confirms the hypothesis suggested by the approximations in Question 1.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q2-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Bob guesses that $[1;2,2,2,\\ldots]$ converges (approaches) to $\\sqrt{2}$ since there are lots of $2$’s. He then claims that $[1;3,3,3,\\ldots]$ must converge to $\\sqrt{3}$ since there are lots of $3$’s. Determine if Bob’s hypothesis is correct or incorrect. If incorrect, state the correct value of $[1;3,3,3,\\ldots]$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Incorrect. $[1;3,3,3,\\ldots]=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{13}-1}{2}$.\n\nUse the repeating tail to write the first denominator as $2+Y$, where $Y$ is the value of the continued fraction.",
          "worked": "Let $Y=[1;3,3,3,\\ldots]$. The reciprocal term repeats, so the first denominator is $3+(Y-1)=Y+2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\nY&=1+\\tfrac{1}{Y+2}\\\\\n(Y-1)(Y+2)&=1\\\\\nY^2+Y-2&=1\\\\\nY^2+Y-3&=0.\n\\end{align*}$$\n\nApply the quadratic formula and take the root greater than 1.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\nY&=\\tfrac{-1+\\sqrt{1+12}}{2}\\\\\n&=\\tfrac{\\sqrt{13}-1}{2}.\n\\end{align*}$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{3}$ into $Y^2+Y-3$ gives $\\sqrt{3}\\ne0$, so Bob’s claim is incorrect.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
